{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Additional evidence: the failing tests LEAK orphaned processes at PPID 1\n\nMeasured immediately after the `make verify` run that produced the 22 failures (2026-10-27 ~23:58 PDT).\n\n`ps -Ao pid,ppid,etime,command` found **32 surviving `bash .../infra/scripts/observability.sh` processes** — 16 parents, each at **PPID 1**, each with one child. Their elapsed times ran from 8 m 19 s to 21 m 41 s, i.e. they had been accumulating steadily through the test run and outlived it.\n\n```\n646     1  16:33  bash .../infra/scripts/observability.sh\n647   646  16:33  bash .../infra/scripts/observability.sh\n6372    1  15:32  ...\n...                      (16 PPID-1 parents in total)\n72536   1  21:41  ...\n```\n\nI reaped all 32 by explicit PID; none remain.\n\n### Why this happens, and why it compounds the bug\n\n`_run` invokes the script with `subprocess.run([\"bash\", str(SCRIPT)], env=env, timeout=60)`. On `TimeoutExpired` Python kills the **direct** child only. The script has already forked its own subshell, which survives, reparents to `init`, and keeps running — unbounded, since nothing else holds a reference to it.\n\nSo each failing case leaves a process behind. With the `/var/lib/rebar` writes failing, every affected case times out, and the run leaks one orphan pair per timeout. On a developer host running repeated verifies these accumulate without limit; AGENTS.md treats a process reaching PPID 1 as the documented orphan class (`docs/orphaned-processes.md`), and `python3 scripts/check_orphaned_load.py` is the detector for exactly this.\n\n### Effect on the acceptance criteria\n\nFixing the `/var/lib/rebar` override gap removes the timeouts and therefore the leak in practice, but it does not fix the leak itself: any future timeout in these tests will orphan again. Add to the plan:\n\n- `_run` must reap the whole process group on timeout, not just the direct child — start the script in its own session/process group and signal that group on `TimeoutExpired`, then `wait` on it.\n- Add an assertion, or a fixture teardown check, that no `observability.sh` process survives the test — so a reintroduced leak fails the suite instead of accumulating silently on the host.\n\nSuggested additional acceptance criterion:\n\n- [ ] After the four observability test files run (including an induced timeout), no `infra/scripts/observability.sh` process survives and none reaches PPID 1."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793169482283871209,"uuid":"2d32173a-7529-4b0a-902d-f00275af6c15"}